# Step 09 — Teardown (full, back to an empty account)
# Account: 759945100724  OCM: stage  Date: 2026-10-12
#
# Order matters. Two traps:
#   1. The cluster must be FULLY uninstalled before the operator roles and
#      OIDC config go: the uninstall USES them to clean up AWS resources.
#      Delete them early and the uninstall wedges half-finished.
#   2. The cluster must be fully gone before the VPC stack, or leftovers in
#      the VPC block delete-stack.

## 1. Cluster  (~12 min)

AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa delete cluster -c alebedev-test --watch -y

# ...
# 2026-10-12 14:11:07 hostedclusters alebedev-test All guest resources destroyed
# INFO: Cluster 'alebedev-test' completed uninstallation
#
# rosa prints the follow-up commands. It listed exactly the 8 operator roles
# and no account role, which confirms `--prefix alebedev` below is safe even
# though the account roles share that prefix.

## 2. Operator roles

AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa delete operator-roles --prefix alebedev -m auto -y
# Deleted all 8 (alebedev-kube-system-*, alebedev-openshift-*). Account roles
# untouched.

## 3. OIDC config

AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa delete oidc-config --oidc-config-id 2td89f2qnstd6p18r7dmj5d93ufr76gs -m auto -y

# USE oidc-config, NOT oidc-provider.
# `rosa delete cluster` suggests:
#     rosa delete oidc-provider --oidc-config-id <id>
# but that is the Classic path -- its help says "Cleans up OIDC provider of
# deleted STS cluster" and it only removes the IAM provider, leaving the
# managed config still registered in OCM. `rosa delete oidc-config` says
# "Cleans up OIDC config based on registered OIDC Config ID" and does both.

## 4. VPC stack  —  FAILS THE FIRST TIME, see below

AWS_PROFILE=saml aws cloudformation delete-stack --region us-west-2 --stack-name alebedev-test-vpc
AWS_PROFILE=saml aws cloudformation wait stack-delete-complete --region us-west-2 --stack-name alebedev-test-vpc

### GOTCHA: DELETE_FAILED on the VPC
#
# Everything deleted except the VPC itself:
#
#   VPC  DELETE_FAILED
#   "The vpc 'vpc-0dbbc83fea9143e44' has dependencies and cannot be deleted"
#
# Cause: a leftover security group
#
#   sg-0633f729a0a847ec0  2td8f1mb90vr0c3jefg9dv89uijh1bn3-vpce-private-router
#
# named after the CLUSTER ID. HCP creates it for the private-router VPC
# endpoint; it is not a CloudFormation resource, so CFN neither knows about it
# nor removes it, and the cluster uninstall leaves it behind. No ENIs, no
# endpoints and no subnets were left -- just this one SG.
#
# Fix:

AWS_PROFILE=saml aws ec2 delete-security-group --region us-west-2 --group-id sg-0633f729a0a847ec0
AWS_PROFILE=saml aws cloudformation delete-stack --region us-west-2 --stack-name alebedev-test-vpc
# -> stack gone, 0 VPCs in us-west-2
#
# To find it next time (skip 'default', it goes with the VPC):
#   aws ec2 describe-security-groups --region us-west-2 \
#     --filters Name=vpc-id,Values=<vpc> \
#     --query "SecurityGroups[?GroupName!='default'].GroupId"
#
# rosa-hcp.sh handles this: on a failed delete-stack it reaps orphaned
# available ENIs and non-default SGs, then retries once.

### Side note: expired SAML credentials
#
# `aws cloudformation wait` also exits non-zero when the AWS session expires
# mid-wait (ExpiredToken) and cannot be told apart from a real failure.
# Deletion continues server-side either way, so re-check the stack state
# rather than trusting the waiter's exit code.

## 5. Account-wide roles
#
# These were deliberately KEPT at first, then removed to leave no footprint in
# a shared account. Keeping them is free and makes the next cluster shorter;
# deleting them returns the account to 'no ROSA cluster can be created here'.

AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa delete account-roles --prefix alebedev --hosted-cp -m auto -y
AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa delete user-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::759945100724:role/alebedev-User-alebedev@redhat.com-Role -m auto -y
AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa delete ocm-role  --role-arn arn:aws:iam::759945100724:role/alebedev-OCM-Role-11009103 -m auto -y

# user-role and ocm-role are unlinked automatically before deletion:
#   WARN: Role ARN '...' is linked to organization '1HAXGgCYqHpednsRDiwWsZBmDlA'
#   INFO: Successfully unlinked role-arn '...'
#   INFO: Successfully deleted the OCM role
#
# Delete ocm-role LAST -- it is what authorizes the other operations.

## Final state — verified

$ aws iam list-roles    --query 'Roles[?starts_with(RoleName,`alebedev`)]'      -> none
$ aws iam list-policies --scope Local --query '...starts_with(`alebedev`)'      -> none
$ aws iam list-open-id-connect-providers                                        -> []
$ aws ec2 describe-vpcs --region us-west-2 | jq '.Vpcs|length'                  -> 0
$ rosa list clusters      -> No clusters available
$ rosa list ocm-role      -> No ocm roles available
$ rosa list user-role     -> No user roles available
$ rosa list account-roles -> no account roles found

# Account 759945100724 is back to its pre-2026-10-12 state. Nothing billing.

## Note on sharing this account
#
# Deleting the ocm-role is only correct because nobody else was using it yet.
# An ocm-role is per-AWS-account and LINKED to one OCM org, so in a shared
# account it becomes shared infrastructure for everyone in that org -- the
# same dynamic that blocked the first attempt on 301721915996, where the
# existing role belonged to the CI org (ext 13849960) and showed LINKED=No
# for Red Hat1 (ext 11009103).
#
# Roles for DIFFERENT orgs coexist fine: the name embeds the org external ID.
# If this account gets shared, create the ocm-role with a neutral prefix
# (e.g. --prefix rosa-hcp) rather than a personal one, so nobody deletes it
# thinking it is somebody's leftover.
